## What the landed theory actually gives us

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean] An interface names a hole, its context and type, token-backed
hypotheses, guard, budget, chooser, and acceptor. A candidate retains its filling, declared supports,
blockers, cost, proposer, and status. Effective supports also include tokens for context variables
the filling actually uses. Tokens have identities and versions.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, judge] Judgment first checks typing, then the interface guard,
then live blockers, then missing supports, then budget. Its statuses distinguish ill-typed or
guard-conflicting candidates, pruning, suspension, uncovered cost, and verification.
These statuses are distinct from the five outcomes of repairing a selected filling.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, provenance; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean]
The provenance representation is a list of positive-support/negative-blocker pairs, one per candidate
passing typing, guard, and cost. Alternatives form a sum; simultaneous supports form a product.
It is independent of which tokens are live, but depends on the environment, candidate set, and checker.

[INFERRED] This is useful signed provenance, not yet an arbitrary symbolic polynomial in all changing
facts. In particular, guard failures are filtered for the supplied environment. Retain the candidate
archive as well as its currently computed polynomial, or a later environment change loses alternatives.
Do not flatten a shared proof DAG into exponentially many monomials merely to print this notation.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, withdraw_never_refutes]
Withdrawal changes support availability, not the type or guard calculation; it does not create a
typing/guard conflict. The ill-typed conflict theorem refutes the proposed filling's typing judgment.
It does not prove that the hole's target proposition is false or that no other filling exists.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, repair_agrees_from_scratch;
N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, observe/fromScratch] Under coherence, repair and fresh judging
produce equal recorded candidate-status lists. They use the same template and retained candidates.
The observation excludes selections, accepted flags, history, and owed obligations.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, locality/repair_rejudged_eq_touched]
An unrelated token edit leaves the closure unchanged, and the repair log contains exactly touched
candidates. Environment replacement touches all candidates. The definitions still scan lists to find
affected entries; the logged-work theorem is not a bound saying elapsed work is O(affected entries).

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, repairSelected; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean]
The five outcomes are kept, alternative, replacement, strengthening, and suspended. An alternative
with the same filling can change the support route; a different replacement is offered for selection.
Strengthening supplies a smaller-context witness; it does not rewrite the closure's template.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, strengthening_sound/session_preserves_tpl]
The strengthening law needs a lawful calculus, the relevant checked filling, and an unused variable.
It reconstructs the original filling by weakening the smaller one. Sessions preserve the template.
Consequently, changing a theorem's telescope is not already one of the existing edit operations.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, Template.Typed/Derivable/Available/Settled]
Derivable means the partially filled template types under the remaining hole declarations.
Availability checks selected supports, and settledness quantifies over selections; an empty selection
set can satisfy these conditions. None alone says every intended obligation has a complete proof.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, conditional_soundness_closure]
The theorem requires a typed template, coherence, and settledness. The operations do not establish
template typing from arbitrary data. This is the same missing producer obligation identified in
W:ACTIVITIES-LOG.md; a proof-engineering adapter must supply it, not erase the premise in its UI.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Closure.lean, executed_prefix_blocks_replan]
Acceptance freezes a selection and preserves settlement obligations even when support disappears.
Version reissue does not revive old citations. The separate Rule/Derives/least-fixpoint theory shows
why reference counting cannot ground a cycle after its last external fact is withdrawn.

[INFERRED] For a proof library, acceptance should freeze a historical receipt at a revision.
It must not forbid replacing that theorem's proof in every future revision. Archive the old accepted
object, open the successor obligation, and retain reconciliation obligations for claims already made.
This gives withdrawal its honest meaning without pretending published history can be rewritten.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Core.lean; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Typing.lean; CoreInstance.lean]
CMTT has explicit contextual metavariables and substitutions; `cmttLaws` discharges the closure laws.
Its object types are simply typed, including contextual boxes. Lean's dependent telescopes, universe
levels, typeclass arguments, inductives, and reduction behavior are not this object calculus.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/LangCalculus.lean; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/LangInstance.lean]
LANG-68's deployed instance uses closed `ref` holes and `fillRefs`. `langLaws` is real, but
`langTemplate_no_hyps` proves typed templates in that fragment have no token-backed hypotheses.
The contextual strengthening path is therefore empty for those templates, unlike the CMTT instance.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Protocol.lean; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/ProtocolTyping.lean]
The protocol theory adds response-indexed continuations, explicit interfaces, reusable facts,
affine resource tokens, and history. `Open.Replan` is an explicit theorem premise; the data setter
alone does not enforce it. Admission of the next operation is separate from protocol derivability.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Protocol.lean, locality/repair_agrees_from_scratch]
Its fragment-repair theorem is especially close to a judge adapter: each fragment has a support list
and a `ReadsOnly` proof. Agreement outside changed keys implies selective repair equals full checking.
The significant obligation for our exporter is proving or conservatively enforcing `ReadsOnly`.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Protocol.lean, Coin; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Protocol.lean]
One filling per shared hole name cannot predict both coin responses. Response-dependent names can
encode foresight; `foresight_iff_names_separate` states that boundary. The preceding assistant's
summary in cv:7c10f132 reversed this direction; the source controls this memo's interpretation.

[INFERRED] In proof orchestration, the response-dependent continuation is a legitimate next task
after Lean reports a goal or counterexample. A proof candidate for a fixed obligation is not allowed
to change its target retrospectively to fit that response. Keep claim identity and revision explicit.

## A theorem as an enduring obligation

[INFERRED] Represent a claim by a stable identity and an immutable obligation revision. The latter
contains its elaborated target, telescope, universe parameters, definition-closure identity,
intended deployment instance, toolchain, and the interpretation of the advertised claim.
A declaration name alone is not an obligation identity: its meaning can change underneath it.

[INFERRED] Each candidate records a proof artifact, a reconstruction recipe, precise support edges,
audit evidence, blockers, and checking receipts. Keep candidate recipes after suspension or pruning.
A failed tactic attempt is useful repair evidence but is not a kernel proof or a refutation.
A timeout remains incomplete work, never an ill-typed certificate produced by interpreting failure as false.

