## Three ways this idea is most likely to be wrong

[INFERRED] Failure 1: the exported support graph is unsound or too coarse to be useful. Constants
miss projections, elaborator inputs, external configuration, or premise instantiations; alternatively
everything depends on everything. A declaration graph can look precise while missing the causal
inputs of the actual checker or the semantic subject of an audit.

[INFERRED] Refuting measurement: run the fixed mutation matrix and held-out historical redesigns,
comparing every reused success with independent fresh checking and exact contract/audit revisions.
One falsely reused success refutes support sufficiency. Record false invalidations against actual
rechecking; if conservative closure regularly approaches the entire library, claimed locality wins fail.

[INFERRED] Failure 2: closure equality proves the wrong thing for dependent redesign. Fixed-template
status repair is correct while the target changed, a premise silently strengthened, or a supposedly
complete obligation still has holes. The existing abstraction may require substantial new theory
before it can justify the cross-revision claims we actually want.

[INFERRED] Refuting measurement: include changed definition meanings, dependent binder deletion,
changed constructor interfaces, and obligation-wide vacuity in the pilot. Compare target revisions,
kernel receipts, open-hole coverage, and residual work with fresh reconstruction. Any admitted claim
whose exact target lacks evidence refutes the bridge; an unexplained residual mismatch refutes repair.

[INFERRED] Failure 3: persistent alternatives cost more than rebuilding the proofs they save.
Most theorems may have only one practical recipe; alternatives may share every fragile support;
provenance expansion, export time, and reviewer bookkeeping may dominate the saved checker work.
That would refute the performance case without refuting the value of a truthful obligation journal.

[INFERRED] Refuting measurement: compare current incremental Lean plus existing manifests, the
journal with one candidate, and the journal retaining alternatives over the same edit corpus.
Charge export, storage, indexing, repair, checking, and human classification time. If alternatives
save no independent re-proof work or increase total cost, keep the journal and redesign retention.

